us03-status-lifecycle passed in this run (it failed in T46 run 1; its cause is below, from the T46 trace).

== Causes and fixes ==
1. WebKit session-isolation "page.goto ... is interrupted by another navigation to /dashboard" (288 and 378; any test that signs in and then navigates).
   The Sign-in link lands on /dashboard. The private layout's TimeZoneCookie (components/time-zone-cookie.tsx) writes the `tz` cookie on mount and calls router.refresh().
   On a busy machine the sign-in helper's `networkidle` came before the dashboard hydrated, so the helper returned and the caller's page.goto started just as that refresh
   fetch was sent. WebKit cancels the in-flight fetch when the new navigation starts; the trace has the page's own console line
   "Failed to fetch RSC payload for http://127.0.0.1:4311/dashboard. Falling back to browser navigation. TypeError: Load failed", 0.27 s after the goto began, and
   Next.js answers a failed RSC fetch with a full navigation to that URL (next/dist/client/components/router-reducer/fetch-server-response.js), which interrupts the goto.
   Not a product bug: a person never navigates in the same tick as the first hydration. Fix: tests/e2e/support/genuine-session.ts now, when the browser's zone is not yet in
   the `tz` cookie, waits for that router.refresh() request (an RSC request that is neither a prefetch nor a server action) to finish, or, if it fails, for the full load
   Next.js does instead. Chromium kept the fetch alive, which is why only WebKit showed it.
2. session-isolation-bfcache 292/311 "the back/forward cache is not live in this run (/privacy: IgnoreEventAndEvict | NetworkRequestRedirected)".
   The control opened /privacy and left it for /terms right after `load`. The root layout's Vercel Analytics and Speed Insights load their scripts only after hydration;
   locally the proxy redirects those requests to sign-in. In the traces /privacy was left 80 ms after the first script request went out (it was redirected while the page sat in
   the cache: NetworkRequestRedirected), or before hydration had finished at all (IgnoreEventAndEvict). Chromium then evicts the page, so the control is right to refuse.
   Fix: the spec waits until the new document has hydrated and both analytics script requests have ended (afterHydrationRequestsEnded) before leaving /privacy, and before
   A leaves the editor (as well as the existing networkidle). No product change.
3. us03-status-lifecycle "Mark as Paid": the row still said Pending after the 5 s expect timeout (T46 run 1). In that trace the Mark as Paid server action POST alone took 4.1 s
   on the loaded VM, and the row check started as soon as the click returned, so its 5 s ran out 0.9 s after the answer arrived, before the row was redrawn. Not a race in the product: the answer arrives, just late.
   Fix: tests/e2e/support/invoice-ui.ts untilServerActionDone() waits for the server action's response to finish before the row is checked; used for Mark as Paid,
   Mark as Pending, Cancel invoice and Duplicate in us03 and Mark as Paid in us05-two-tabs.
Guard: playwright.config.ts now sets `workers: process.env.CI ? 2 : undefined`, so `CI=1 pnpm test:e2e` (the README's command) and the nightly (now plain `pnpm test:e2e`) run
the same load. The fixes were proven first at 5 workers, the load that failed before.

